      <KpiStrip kpis={event.kpis} />

      <section id="what-happened" data-layer="fact">
        <SectionKicker text="The lineup changed" />
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Macklemore was removed from eight scheduled appearances on Ed Sheeran's stadium tour, the Loop Tour, on September 14. Macklemore said Sheeran and his team made the decision; tour promoter Messina Touring Group said venues had told it they would not allow concerts with Macklemore on the lineup.<Cite s="macklemore-removal-statement-2026-09-14" passage="Ed Sheeran and his team have made the decision to remove me from The Loop tour." /><Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="we have been notified by venues on the upcoming U.S. tour dates that they will not allow a concert to take place with Macklemore on the lineup" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Ten North American shows remained on Sheeran's official schedule after September 14, but Macklemore had been scheduled for eight of them. He had already appeared at both MetLife Stadium dates, on September 4 and 5. The Loop Tour continued without him: its first date after the removal went ahead in Philadelphia on September 19, where Sheeran began alone with no support act.<Cite s="loop-tour-north-america-faq" passage="September 19, 2026    Philadelphia, PA" /><Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="Macklemore was slated to appear at eight of the 10 shows left" /><Cite s="ed-sheeran-past-tour-dates-2026-09-20" passage="05 Sep 2026 MetLife Stadium" /><Cite s="ed-sheeran-past-tour-dates-2026-09-20" passage="04 Sep 2026 MetLife Stadium" /><Cite s="ap-philadelphia-outcome-2026-09-19" passage="Sheeran began the concert alone on stage with no opener" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The next day, Finneas, Aaron Rowe, Lukas Graham and Beoga announced that they were leaving. They did not all hold the same job: Rowe and Lukas Graham had been replacement U.S. support acts, Beoga had played during Sheeran's set, and Finneas was booked for the South American leg.<Cite s="ap-support-acts-2026-09-15" passage="Rowe and Lukas Graham would have been replacements for Macklemore on Sheeran’s remaining U.S. dates, while Beoga had been performing several songs" /><Cite s="ap-support-acts-2026-09-15" passage="Finneas, who is Billie Eilish’s brother and musical collaborator, was due to tour with Sheeran in South America later this year" /></p>

	        <p data-layer="narrative">AP asked three music-business participants who were not involved in Sheeran's tour how a stadium tour normally works. They said a promoter finances the production, pays the acts and deals with venues; a venue can refuse a lineup; and a stadium-level headliner generally has substantial approval over the show.<Cite s="ap-tour-power-2026-09-17" passage="Neither Gary nor Waddell nor Serwin are involved in the Sheeran tour." /><Cite s="ap-tour-power-2026-09-17" passage="The promoter puts up the money, pays all of the acts, deals with the venue." /><Cite s="ap-tour-power-2026-09-17" passage="Certainly they can lock the doors" /><Cite s="ap-tour-power-2026-09-17" passage="at the end of the day that superstar headliner is going to have approval over everything" /></p>

	        <p data-layer="narrative">Sheeran said support acts chose their own setlists.<Cite s="ed-sheeran-response-2026-09-15" passage="As with all my support acts, I agree that they play a setlist of their choosing." /> He said venues threatened to pull shows after Macklemore's performances and that he spent the week speaking with them, including Robert Kraft, owner of the New England Patriots and Gillette Stadium, but could not change the venue and promoter decision.<Cite s="usa-today-kraft-2026-09-14" passage="Robert Kraft, owner of the New England Patriots and their stadium" /><Cite s="ed-sheeran-response-2026-09-15" passage="I spoke with the venues at length all week to try to build a bridge, including with Robert Kraft, but the venue's and promoter's decision was final." /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Sheeran said Macklemore's contract was with the promoter, not him. He said he would not abandon fans, crew, support acts or musicians who relied on him for work, and that his audience included children and families who did not expect a political forum.<Cite s="ed-sheeran-response-2026-09-15" passage="Macklemore's contract for the tour was with the promoter, not me." /><Cite s="ed-sheeran-response-2026-09-15" passage="I would never let down the fans who had made plans, nor would I abandon the touring crew and the other support acts and musicians who rely on me for work and to make a living." /><Cite s="ed-sheeran-response-2026-09-15" passage="My audience includes children and families, and they do not expect a political forum when they come to my shows." /></p>

        <DataTable columns={["Date", "City", "Venue"]} rows={remainingDateRows} caption="Sheeran's 10 remaining North American dates after September 14" />

        <SourcedBlock source="loop-tour-north-america-faq" kind="record" detail>
	          <p>There were {seriesReceipt.sourceRows} North American shows on the September 2025 schedule still posted on September 20. Ten of those dates fall after September 14.<Cite s="loop-tour-north-america-faq" passage="September 19, 2026    Philadelphia, PA" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="ed-sheeran-past-tour-dates-2026-09-20" kind="record" detail>
          <p>MetLife Stadium hosted Sheeran and Macklemore on September 4 and September 5.<Cite s="ed-sheeran-past-tour-dates-2026-09-20" passage="05 Sep 2026 MetLife Stadium" /><Cite s="ed-sheeran-past-tour-dates-2026-09-20" passage="04 Sep 2026 MetLife Stadium" /><Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="performing two shows at MetLife Stadium outside of New York City, on Sept. 4 and 5." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="why-removed" data-layer="fact">
        <SectionKicker text="MetLife to venue objections" />
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Why Macklemore was removed</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">At the first MetLife show, Macklemore said “Free Palestine,” displayed images of destruction in Gaza and performed “Hind's Hall.”<Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="At the first show, the Seattle rapper yelled “Free Palestine” onstage and expressed solidarity with people in Gaza and the occupied West Bank. He also showed images from the devastation in Gaza and performed his protest song, “Hind’s Hall,”" /></p>

	        <p data-layer="narrative">The American Jewish Committee, an advocacy group that criticized the performance, said its New Jersey office contacted the Meadowlands after the first show. AJC said stadium administrators raised its concerns directly with the tour and artists and offered refunds before the second show to ticket holders who chose not to attend; MetLife had not commented on AJC's account by September 20.<Cite s="ajc-metlife-intervention-2026-09-08" passage="AJC New Jersey immediately contacted the Meadowlands to express our community’s outrage." /><Cite s="ajc-metlife-intervention-2026-09-08" passage="The stadium administration raised these concerns directly with the tour and artists, and offered refunds to ticket holders before the second show who chose not to attend." /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Macklemore repeated his call for a “Free Palestine” at the second MetLife show.<Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="At the second MetLife show, Macklemore reiterated his calls for a “Free Palestine”" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The first performance drew criticism from pro-Israel groups. The Israeli American Council addressed its petition to Sheeran, tour leadership and every venue hosting the tour, called for Macklemore's removal, and asked whether his speech, visuals and “Hind's Hall” had been approved in advance.<Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="This spurred much criticism and backlash from pro-Israel groups." /><Cite s="iac-loop-tour-petition-2026-09" passage="We call on Ed Sheeran, tour leadership, and the leadership of all venues hosting the Tour to:" /><Cite s="iac-loop-tour-petition-2026-09" passage="Remove Macklemore from the remaining LOOP Tour dates on which he is scheduled to appear." /><Cite s="iac-loop-tour-petition-2026-09" passage="We also call on tour leadership to clarify whether Macklemore's political speech, visual content, and performance of Hind's Hall were approved in advance" /></p>

	        <p data-layer="narrative">Kraft confirmed one venue decision. He said Gillette Stadium determined that Macklemore's participation on September 25 and 26 would cross its line against providing a platform for hate speech, citing Macklemore's recent actions, material from the MetLife Stadium stage and what Kraft described as a broader history of antisemitic rhetoric and imagery.<Cite s="usa-today-kraft-2026-09-14" passage="we determined that his participation in the September 25th and 26th concerts at Gillette Stadium would cross that line" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Macklemore said Sheeran maintained an “I don't take sides” position. He said he had made the same statements on stages for almost three years and argued that their association with a larger artist, major U.S. stadiums and bigger audiences made them newly risky.<Cite s="macklemore-removal-statement-2026-09-14" passage={'he couldn\'t get past his public facing, "I don\'t take sides."'} /><Cite s="macklemore-removal-statement-2026-09-14" passage="What I said at MetLife I have been saying on stages around the world for almost three years." /><Cite s="macklemore-removal-statement-2026-09-14" passage="Because now I'm sharing a stage with one of the biggest artists in the world, in some of the biggest stadiums in America." /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Macklemore said antisemitism was real, but argued that criticism of Israel or opposition to Zionism should not be conflated with hatred of Jewish people.<Cite s="macklemore-removal-statement-2026-09-14" passage="Antisemitism is real." /><Cite s="macklemore-removal-statement-2026-09-14" passage={'When criticizing Israel, opposing Zionism or saying "Free Palestine" gets conflated with hatred of Jewish people'} /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Macklemore said Sheeran told him that Kraft had rallied other stadium owners and described “Free Palestine” and the Palestinian flag as hurtful. Kraft confirmed the Gillette refusal but did not address Macklemore's allegation of wider coordination.<Cite s="macklemore-removal-statement-2026-09-14" passage="Ed also told me that Kraft had rallied some of the other stadium owners" /><Cite s="macklemore-removal-statement-2026-09-14" passage={'He told me that the words "Free Palestine" and the image of the Palestinian flag were hurtful to a lot of people he spoke with.'} /><Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="Kraft, however, did not address the claim that Kraft also “rallied” other stadium owners" /></p>

	        <p data-layer="narrative">Gillette Stadium's owner confirmed that it would not allow Macklemore to perform there.<Cite s="usa-today-kraft-2026-09-14" passage="we determined that his participation in the September 25th and 26th concerts at Gillette Stadium would cross that line" /> Alan Clendenin, a Tampa Sports Authority board member and Tampa City Council chair, said the lineup change had nothing to do with the public agency that manages Raymond James Stadium.<Cite s="tampa-bay-times-raymond-james-response-2026-09-15" passage={'One member, Tampa City Council chairperson Alan Clendenin, said the change "doesn\'t have anything to do with the Tampa Sports Authority."'} /> Messina said venues had objected.<Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="we have been notified by venues on the upcoming U.S. tour dates that they will not allow a concert to take place with Macklemore on the lineup" /> Rolling Stone reported that Lincoln Financial Field, Mercedes-Benz Stadium, Lucas Oil Stadium, Bank of America Stadium and AT&T Stadium objected; none of those five venues had confirmed its account by September 20.<Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="The venues that allegedly objected to the rapper include: Lincoln Financial Field in Philadelphia; Gillette Stadium in Foxborough, Massachusetts; Mercedes-Benz Stadium in Atlanta; Lucas Oil Stadium in Indianapolis; Bank of America Stadium in Charlotte, North Carolina; AT&T Stadium in Arlington, Texas; and Raymond James Stadium in Tampa, Florida." /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Bank of America Stadium and Gillette Stadium were the only two on that seven-venue list that Rolling Stone identified as privately owned. The other five were owned or operated by cities, states or other public bodies.<Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="the rest are owned and, in many cases, operated by cities, states, or other government entities" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="gillette-loop-tour-2026-09-20" kind="record" detail>
          <p>At Gillette Stadium, stadium or artist management reserved the right to bar signs or banners because of their message content.<Cite s="gillette-loop-tour-2026-09-20" passage="Stadium/artist management reserves the right to prohibit signs or banners due to message content." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="wsj-tour-phone-call-2026-09-16" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The Wall Street Journal reported, citing unnamed sources, that Carolina Panthers owner David Tepper, Atlanta Falcons owner Arthur Blank and Philadelphia Eagles owner Jeffrey Lurie joined the pressure. Their communications and the tour contracts remain private.<Cite s="wsj-tour-phone-call-2026-09-16" passage="More than that, several other National Football League owners were also ready to cancel Sheeran’s dates as long as Macklemore remained on the bill." /><Cite s="wsj-tour-phone-call-2026-09-16" passage="David Tepper and Arthur Blank" /><Cite s="wsj-tour-phone-call-2026-09-16" passage="Philadelphia Eagles owner Jeffrey Lurie would soon reach the same conclusion" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <p>The four artists who left held different tour roles.</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>Who held final authority remains unresolved.</h3>
              <p>Macklemore attributed the removal to Sheeran and his team. Sheeran attributed it to the promoter and said the venues and promoter made the final decision. Messina said venues would not accept the lineup. The industry participants interviewed by AP were not involved in this tour, and no executed artist, promoter or venue contract is public.<Cite s="macklemore-removal-statement-2026-09-14" passage="Ed Sheeran and his team have made the decision to remove me from The Loop tour." /><Cite s="ed-sheeran-response-2026-09-15" passage="Macklemore coming off tour was the promoter's decision, it was not mine." /><Cite s="rolling-stone-removal-2026-09-14" passage="we have been notified by venues on the upcoming U.S. tour dates" /><Cite s="ap-tour-power-2026-09-17" passage="Neither Gary nor Waddell nor Serwin are involved in the Sheeran tour." /></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Four artists left, but they were not four remaining openers.</h3>
              <p>Rowe and Lukas Graham were replacement U.S. support acts. Beoga described itself as Sheeran's band for the remaining U.S. tour. Finneas was scheduled for South America later in the year.<Cite s="ap-support-acts-2026-09-15" passage="Rowe and Lukas Graham would have been replacements for Macklemore on Sheeran’s remaining U.S. dates" /><Cite s="beoga-withdrawal-2026-09-15" passage="depart as Ed Sheeran's band on the remaining US Loop tour" /><Cite s="ap-support-acts-2026-09-15" passage="Finneas, who is Billie Eilish’s brother and musical collaborator, was due to tour with Sheeran in South America later this year" /></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="why-others-left" data-layer="fact">
        <SectionKicker text="Four decisions, four roles" />
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Why the other artists left</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Finneas said artists should not be silenced when they speak for oppressed people and withdrew from his upcoming dates with Sheeran.<Cite s="finneas-withdrawal-2026-09-15" passage="Artists must not be silenced when they speak up for the oppressed. I have decided to withdraw from my upcoming tour dates with Ed Sheeran." /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Aaron Rowe withdrew as a North American support act. He said he could not accept billionaires using their power to silence people who speak up.<Cite s="aaron-rowe-withdrawal-2026-09-15" passage="I have made the decision to withdraw as a support act for Ed Sheeran's The Loop North American Tour." /><Cite s="aaron-rowe-withdrawal-2026-09-15" passage="I cannot stand by and allow billionaires to use their position of power to silence the rightful voices of those who speak up" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Beoga departed as Sheeran's band for the remaining U.S. dates. The band said it would not play venues that silence artists for saying “Free Palestine.”<Cite s="beoga-withdrawal-2026-09-15" passage="We have made the decision to depart as Ed Sheeran's band on the remaining US Loop tour" /><Cite s="beoga-withdrawal-2026-09-15" passage="we will not play in venues that silence artists for saying Free Palestine" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Lukas Graham objected to wealth determining who gets to speak and withdrew from the remaining dates. The band also said there was not one single reason for its decision.<Cite s="lukas-graham-withdrawal-2026-09-15" passage="Nobody's bank balance should decide who gets to speak or which suffering we're allowed to acknowledge." /><Cite s="lukas-graham-withdrawal-2026-09-15" passage="We're withdrawing from the remaining dates. There isn't one single reason behind this decision." /></p>

        <DataTable columns={["Artist", "Scheduled role", "Recorded action"]} rows={lineupRows} caption="Four artists, their tour roles and what each did" />

        <SourcedBlock source="rolling-stone-aaron-rowe-followup-2026-09-18" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Rowe later said the criticism should focus on Kraft rather than Sheeran, while maintaining that Macklemore should not have been removed.<Cite s="rolling-stone-aaron-rowe-followup-2026-09-18" passage="when you really should be focusing on the likes of [New England Patriots owner] Robert Kraft." /><Cite s="rolling-stone-aaron-rowe-followup-2026-09-18" passage="Rowe affirmed that he didn’t think Macklemore should’ve been pulled off the tour" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="what-happened-next" data-layer="fact">
        <SectionKicker text="Philadelphia" />
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened next</h2>

	        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 17, CAIR-Philadelphia announced that it had submitted a Pennsylvania Right-to-Know request concerning the Philadelphia lineup decision.<Cite s="cair-philadelphia-records-request-2026-09-17" passage="today announced that it has submitted a Pennsylvania Right-to-Know Law request seeking records concerning the decision" /></p>

	        <p data-layer="narrative">CAIR-Philadelphia said its request did not presume what any records would show. Pennsylvania's five-business-day response period had not run by September 20.<Cite s="cair-philadelphia-records-request-2026-09-17" passage="the request does not presume what those records will show" /><Cite s="pa-open-records-citizens-guide-2026-09-20" passage="An Agency has five business days to respond in writing to: (1) grant the request; (2) deny the request, citing the legal basis for the denial or partial denial; or (3) invoke a 30-calendar day extension for certain reasons." /></p>

	        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 18, the Massachusetts attorney general's office told Boston 25 it was reviewing at least 25 complaints requesting Gillette Stadium refunds from Ticketmaster. That number counts complaints, not purchasers. The office said changes to the lineup “do not necessarily entitle ticket purchasers to a refund.”<Cite s="boston-25-gillette-refund-complaints-2026-09-18" passage="they’re reviewing at least 25 complaints requesting refunds from Ticketmaster" /><Cite s="boston-25-gillette-refund-complaints-2026-09-18" passage="changes to the lineup do not necessarily entitle ticket purchasers to a refund" /></p>

	        <p data-layer="narrative">Ticketmaster told Boston 25 that organizers set refund policy. The tour FAQ says, “All sales are final.”<Cite s="boston-25-gillette-refund-complaints-2026-09-18" passage="refund policy is up to the organizers" /><Cite s="loop-tour-north-america-faq" passage="All sales are final." /></p>

	        <p data-layer="narrative">Sheeran opened by saying he had made mistakes during the week and apologizing for disappointing fans. He called the situation in Gaza “catastrophic and unjustifiable” and said he worried about venues preapproving performances.<Cite s="nyt-philadelphia-outcome-2026-09-19" passage="he said he had made “mistakes” during the week and apologized for disappointing fans." /><Cite s="guardian-sheeran-philadelphia-video-2026-09-20" passage="catastrophic and unjustifiable" /><Cite s="ap-philadelphia-outcome-2026-09-19" passage="He also told fans he worries about venues preapproving performances." /></p>

        <DataTable columns={["When", "Record"]} rows={chronologyRows} />

        <SourcedBlock source="ap-philadelphia-outcome-2026-09-19" kind="record" detail>
          <p>A small group of pro-Palestinian demonstrators gathered outside before the Philadelphia concert.<Cite s="ap-philadelphia-outcome-2026-09-19" passage="Before Saturday’s show, a small group of pro-Palestinian demonstrators congregated outside the stadium." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="cair-philadelphia-records-request-2026-09-17" kind="record" detail>
          <p>CAIR-Philadelphia's September 17 announcement said it had submitted a request concerning the lineup decision and that the request did not presume what any records would show.<Cite s="cair-philadelphia-records-request-2026-09-17" passage="today announced that it has submitted a Pennsylvania Right-to-Know Law request" /><Cite s="cair-philadelphia-records-request-2026-09-17" passage="the request does not presume what those records will show" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
	        <SourcedBlock source="philadelphia-stadium-lease-2007" kind="record" detail>
	          <p>CAIR-Philadelphia tied its records effort to the stadium's public land: Philadelphia leases the parcel to its industrial-development authority, which leases all or part of the Eagles premises to the team.<Cite s="cair-philadelphia-records-request-2026-09-17" passage="a major venue operating on publicly owned property" /><Cite s="philadelphia-stadium-lease-2007" passage="the City leases to the Authority a parcel of land in connection with the development of a new football stadium for the Eagles." /><Cite s="philadelphia-stadium-lease-2007" passage="the Authority will lease all or a portion of the Eagles Premises to the Eagles." /></p>
	        </SourcedBlock>
	        <SourcedBlock source="nyt-philadelphia-outcome-2026-09-19" kind="record" detail>
	          <p>The Philadelphia show started two hours later than scheduled. Sheeran also called the October 7 attack horrific and said the injustice in the West Bank could not be overlooked.<Cite s="nyt-philadelphia-outcome-2026-09-19" passage="the show’s start time was pushed back two hours." /><Cite s="nyt-philadelphia-outcome-2026-09-19" passage="He called the Oct. 7 Hamas attack “horrific.”" /><Cite s="nyt-philadelphia-outcome-2026-09-19" passage="And the systemic injustice we’re seeing unfold in the West Bank, it cannot be overlooked." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="ap-donations-2026-09-19" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On September 16, Macklemore pledged $1 million from his Loop Tour earnings to six Palestinian relief nonprofits. Kraft separately said he would match Sheeran's $2 million aid pledge, but did not name the recipients.<Cite s="ap-donations-2026-09-19" passage="$1 million pledge from rapper Macklemore" /><Cite s="ap-donations-2026-09-19" passage="Macklemore committed to donate his net earnings from Ed Sheeran’s Loop Tour" /><Cite s="ap-donations-2026-09-19" passage="The six donation recipients" /><Cite s="ap-donations-2026-09-19" passage="he would match Sheeran’s own $2 million donation for aid" /><Cite s="ap-donations-2026-09-19" passage="The post did not specify the gift’s recipients." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="unknowns" data-layer="fact">
        <SectionKicker text="What remains private" />
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative" data-absence>As of September 20, no executed artist, promoter or venue contract was public.</p>

        <p data-layer="narrative" data-absence>Communications among Kraft, Messina, Sheeran's representatives and each venue were not public. It was not known whether Kraft coordinated broader venue pressure or who made the final decisions at Lincoln Financial Field, Mercedes-Benz Stadium, Lucas Oil Stadium, Bank of America Stadium and AT&T Stadium.</p>

	        <p data-layer="narrative" data-absence>No rebuilt support lineup for the September 25 through November 7 dates was public by September 20.</p>

	        <p data-layer="narrative">Massachusetts law requires licensed ticket sellers to guarantee a refund when a ticket “fails to conform to its description as advertised,” unless the purchaser approved a substitution.<Cite s="massachusetts-ticket-refund-law-185d-2026-09-20" passage="the ticket fails to conform to its description as advertised unless the purchaser has pre-approved a substitution of tickets" /> Whether removing an opening act met that test remained unresolved while the attorney general's office reviewed the complaints.<Cite s="boston-25-gillette-refund-complaints-2026-09-18" passage="they’re reviewing at least 25 complaints requesting refunds from Ticketmaster" /></p>
      </section>

      <StorySources event={event.slug} />
      <StoryState view={state} supporting />
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>}
